America: WW1 what happened. The History.

America’s entry into the First World War was the result of mounting pressures that finally overcame deep-seated isolationism. When war erupted in Europe in August 1914, President Woodrow Wilson proclaimed strict neutrality. Most Americans, still wary after the costly Spanish-American War and focused on domestic progressive reforms, saw no reason to join a distant European quarrel. Yet economic realities quickly complicated that stance. American banks and industries extended vast credits and sold munitions primarily to Britain and France, binding the United States more closely to the Allied cause.

German submarine warfare proved the decisive irritant. In February 1915 Germany declared the waters around the British Isles a war zone. The sinking of the British liner Lusitania in May 1915, with 128 American lives lost, produced outrage but not war. Wilson demanded that Germany restrict its U-boat campaign, and for a time Berlin complied. By early 1917, however, Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare, calculating that it could starve Britain into submission before American intervention mattered. In the first months of that campaign, several American merchant ships were torpedoed.

The final catalyst arrived in the form of the Zimmermann Telegram. In January 1917 German Foreign Secretary Arthur Zimmermann secretly offered Mexico an alliance: if the United States entered the war, Germany would help Mexico recover Texas, New Mexico, and Arizona. British intelligence intercepted and published the message, inflaming American public opinion. On 2 April 1917 Wilson asked Congress for a declaration of war, framing the conflict as a crusade to make the world “safe for democracy.” Four days later Congress complied. Thus a combination of unrestricted U-boat attacks, economic entanglement, and the Zimmermann intrigue dragged a reluctant United States into the Great War.

